L219: We evaluate our method across three distinct benchmark suites: the Atari 26 subset used by cite61†Schwarzer et al. (2021) for evaluating generalization in visually complex environments, and the MinAtar (cite63†Young and Tian, 2019 ) and the Octax (cite64†Radji et al., 2026 ) suites for lightweight validation.
L220: In addition to mean and median, we also report the Inter-Quartile Mean (IQM) of our aggregate results with a confidence interval of 95% (cite101†Agarwal et al., 2021 ), computed over all environment and seed runs for each algorithm.
L221: We aim to answer three main questions: (1) Does adding SPR improve the sample efficiency and final performance of streaming agents? (2) Does our orthogonalization strategy successfully reconcile SPR with the ObGD optimizer? (3) Does the auxiliary loss actually result in better representation quality as hypothesized?
L222: ### 5.1 Performance on Standard Environments
L223: We compare three base algorithms, Streaming DQN (dqn), QRC($\lambda$) (qrc), and Stream Q($\lambda$) (strq), against their SPR-augmented counterparts. We use the same set of parameters as cite61†Schwarzer et al. (2021) with additional justification in Table cite94†3 . We integrate this framework into our three streaming baselines, as detailed in Sec. cite17†4.1 . Everywhere in the results, spr, orth, and orth^{2} indicate the application of SPR, orthogonal SPR gradient projection (Sec.
L224: cite18†4.2 ), and orthogonal SPR gradient projection with respect to ObGD gradients after orth (Sec. cite19†4.3 ) respectively.
L225: Table cite88†1 presents the main results of our work. We see that QRC($\lambda$) with SPR and streaming DQN with SPR consistently outperforms the base algorithm over all environment suites and metrics, with the addition of orth providing even higher returns. The learning curves in Figure cite102†3 corroborate this, showing the higher sampling efficiency of SPR-augmented agents through faster learning in the early stages of training.
L226: However, as hypothesized in Section cite19†4.3 , naively adding SPR to Stream Q($\lambda$) leads to performance degradation due to gradient conflicts (see Fig. cite95†4 and Table cite88†1 ). However, applying our orth^{2} strategy successfully recovers performance, surpassing the baseline Stream Q($\lambda$) on all metrics at 100M frames on Atari.
L227: We note that QRC($\lambda$) performs relatively worse on Atari compared to MinAtar and Octax, even though we use the same set of hyperparameters for all experiments (Appendix cite30†A.1 ). A breakdown of per-environment results are present in Tables cite97†7 , cite98†8 , cite99†9 and cite100†10 in Appendix cite40†B . A key constraint of the streaming setting is resource efficiency. Despite the addition of the dynamics model and projection heads, our method still remains computationally feasible (see Appendix cite38†A.9 ).
